KNOWNBUG
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ direct call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ call from a template: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [dcl.fct.default]/1,4: the default argument on the first declaration
of the function template f applies to calls that follow its definition,
which redeclares f without it.  The front end does not use it: the direct
call is "found no match for symbol 'f'", and inside the template g the body
is dropped silently ("C++ front-end dropped the body of function 'g'").  For
a non-template function it works.  libstdc++ 13 declares
__format::__do_vformat_to(_Out, basic_string_view, const
basic_format_args &, const locale * = nullptr) before defining it, and
vformat_to calls it with three arguments, so the bodies of vformat_to
(format:3759/3765) are dropped in every program including <format>
(cpp20_format_header, cpp11_libstdcxx_header_drops/test_format.desc);
passing nullptr explicitly in a header overlay removes those drops and that
of _M_write (format:2456), and exposes _M_visit (3124) and _M_format_arg
(3643), which the restored vformat_to instantiates.
Pre-existing (bin53).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program
(-std=c++98), and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
